          en: "Have you ever wondered where rain comes from? The answer is the water cycle, one of the most important processes on Earth. Every day, the sun heats the water in oceans, lakes, and rivers. This heat turns liquid water into a gas called water vapor, which rises into the sky. Scientists call this step evaporation. As the vapor goes higher, it meets cold air and turns back into tiny drops of water. Millions of these drops join together to form clouds. When the clouds become too heavy, the water falls back to the ground as rain or snow. This step is called precipitation. The water then flows into streams, and the streams flow into rivers, which finally carry it back to the sea. Some of the water soaks into the soil and becomes groundwater, which we can pump out for drinking and farming. Plants also take in water through their roots and release it into the air from their leaves. This process is known as transpiration. The water cycle never stops, and the same water has been traveling around the Earth for billions of years. The water you drank this morning may once have been part of a dinosaur's pond. That is both strange and wonderful, and it reminds us that water is a gift that we should never waste.",
